{"paper_id": "paper-ca74ee97a9759f9f", "doi": "10.1021/acs.jmedchem.6c00260", "title": "Configurationally Locked Ridaifen-B Analogs as Potent Ebola Virus Entry Inhibitors.", "analysis": {"analysis": {"research_question_and_background": "Ebola virus disease poses a significant global public health threat with high mortality rates and limited treatment options, creating a need for effective antiviral therapies targeting the viral glycoprotein. The supplied evidence does not report the specific research question or background for Marburg virus.", "study_design_and_population": "The supplied evidence does not report the specific study design, setting, dates, centres, sample size, or study population.", "methods": "The study involved the design, synthesis, and evaluation of a series of cyclized ridaifen-B-based inhibitors along with site-directed mutagenesis to examine inhibitor binding. The supplied evidence does not detail specific methods for Marburg virus testing.", "main_results": "Several compounds exhibited nanomolar potency in inhibiting Ebola virus glycoprotein-mediated entry and submicromolar potency against Marburg virus entry, with site-directed mutagenesis indicating binding at the Ebola virus GP1/GP2 fusion loop.", "interpretation_and_novelty": "The findings highlight the potential of 1,2-dihydronaphthalene and tetrahydronaphthalene derivatives as valuable leads for developing potent antifiloviral agents targeting filoviruses.", "scientific_and_public_health_significance": "The compounds displayed favorable in vitro pharmacokinetic parameters and encouraged in vivo rapid pharmacokinetic testing for hit compound 41, supporting future antifiloviral drug development.", "limitations_and_evidence_strength": "The supplied evidence does not report detailed methodological limitations, bias, or formal evidence strength assessments, resulting in a low confidence evaluation due to the lack of complete data in the provided text."}, "summary_en": "This study reports the design, synthesis, and evaluation of cyclized ridaifen-B-based inhibitors as antiviral agents against filoviruses. Several compounds showed nanomolar potency inhibiting Ebola virus glycoprotein-mediated entry and submicromolar potency against Marburg virus entry. Site-directed mutagenesis indicated that these inhibitors bind at the viral glycoprotein fusion loop. The compounds also displayed favorable in vitro pharmacokinetic parameters, encouraging further in vivo pharmacokinetic testing for hit compound 41. These findings highlight 1,2-dihydronaphthalene and tetrahydronaphthalene derivatives as promising leads for developing potent antifiloviral therapeutics.", "evidence_ids": {"research_question_and_background": ["A1", "A2"], "study_design_and_population": ["A3"], "methods": ["A3", "A5"], "main_results": ["A4", "A5"], "interpretation_and_novelty": ["A7"], "scientific_and_public_health_significance": ["A6"], "limitations_and_evidence_strength": ["A7"]}, "confidence": "low", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "research", "provider": "gemini", "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "retry_index": 0, "at": "2026-07-22T13:49:09+00:00", "system_chars": 6814, "prompt_chars": 6825, "status": "success", "response_model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "elapsed_ms": 2439, "parser_audit": {"policy_version": "v17.2-flexible-json-parser-1", "method": "direct_json", "response_chars": 2981, "repaired": false, "truncated_signal": false}, "normalization_audit": {"policy_version": "v17.2-prevalidation-normalization-1", "repairs": [], "warnings"