{"paper_id": "paper-661d0bf746e58ed7", "doi": "10.5812/iji-169286", "title": "Digital Health for Pandemic Preparedness: A Critical Review of Innovations, Equity Gaps, and Governance in Infectious Disease Surveillance", "analysis": {"analysis": {"scope_and_question": "The review examines digital health innovations for infectious disease surveillance, focusing on equity gaps and governance within pandemic preparedness. The central question concerns the role of digital health technologies in surveillance, detection, forecasting, contact tracing, governance, and equity.", "evidence_base_and_review_method": "This is a structured thematic narrative review of peer-reviewed and grey literature published from January 2010 to March 2025, following PRISMA 2020 and PRISMA-ScR guidelines. Searches were conducted in PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science from February 5 to 18, 2025, using keywords covering digital-health technologies, infectious diseases, and public-health functions. Records were deduplicated in EndNote X20 and screened by two blinded reviewers (Cohen κ = 0.81). Empirical studies were appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool 2018, and reviews were appraised using Joanna Briggs Institute checklists.", "consensus_and_key_conclusions": "Of 1498 records, 142 studies met inclusion criteria, with 48% rated as high quality. The evidence base is regionally skewed, with 41% from high-income countries and 19% from sub-Saharan Africa or South Asia. No technology was universally superior; AI studies were concentrated in high-income countries, while SMS mHealth studies were concentrated in low- and middle-income countries. Across the included studies, digital health is necessary but not sufficient for pandemic preparedness. Equity, sustainability, and accountability are constituents of effectiveness rather than obstacles.", "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": "The evidence base is heavily skewed regionally, with significant underrepresentation from sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia. Recurring barriers include algorithmic bias, fragmented interoperability, donor-dependent sustainability, and slow benefit-sharing of pathogen data with LMICs. The binding constraint is increasingly political and institutional will rather than analytic uncertainty.", "research_and_practice_implications": "Priorities should include addressing algorithmic bias, improving interoperability, ensuring donor-independent sustainability, and establishing equitable benefit-sharing mechanisms for pathogen data. Governance frameworks must be strengthened to ensure accountability and political commitment."}, "summary_en": "This review assesses digital health innovations for infectious disease surveillance, focusing on equity gaps and governance in pandemic preparedness. A thematic narrative review of 142 studies from 2010 to 2025 found that digital health is necessary but not sufficient for preparedness, with no technology universally superior. The evidence base is regionally skewed, with 41% from high-income countries and 19% from sub-Saharan Africa or South Asia. Recurring barriers include algorithmic bias, fragmented interoperability, donor-dependent sustainability, and slow benefit-sharing of pathogen data with LMICs. The binding constraint is political and institutional will rather than analytic uncertainty. Implications include prioritizing equity, sustainability, and accountability in digital health implementation.", "evidence_ids": {"scope_and_question": ["A1"], "evidence_base_and_review_method": ["A3", "A4", "A5", "A7", "A8"], "consensus_and_key_conclusions": ["A10", "A11", "A12", "A13", "A15", "A16"], "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": ["A11", "A14", "A17"], "research_and_practice_implications": ["A14", "A16"]}, "confidence": "low", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language